   "outcomes": [
    "Analyze the historical experiences of Chicana/o/x peoples from indigenous roots through Spanish colonization, Mexican independence, U.S. conquest, and contemporary times, demonstrating understanding of continuity and change over time",
    "Apply intersectional frameworks and decolonial methodologies to examine how race, class, gender, sexuality, citizenship status, and other social categories shape Chicana/o/x experiences and identities.",
    "Interpret and evaluate diverse forms of Chicana/o/x cultural production including literature, visual arts, music, film, and performance, recognizing their role in resistance, identity formation, and community building.",
    "Conduct research using appropriate academic sources and community-based methodologies, and effectively communicate findings through written, oral, and multimedia formats using discipline-specific terminology.",
    "Examine contemporary issues affecting Chicana/o/x and Latina/o/x communities (including education, immigration, labor, health, and environmental justice) and evaluate strategies for social transformation and community empowerment.",
    "Compare and contrast the Chicana/o/x experience with other racial/ethnic groups in the United States and analyze transnational connections with Mexico, Latin America, and global indigenous movements."
   ],
